25. Board of Governors.―(1) The general supervision and control of the affairs of the University and its powers to lay down policies shall vest in the Board consisting of the following, namely:―
(a) The Chancellor who shall be the Chairperson of the Board of Governors;

(b) The Vice-Chancellor;

(c) The Pro Vice-Chancellor;

(d) one member of the Government not below the rank of Joint Secretary from the concerned Ministry;

(e) four persons from society at large, preferably two or at least one female being persons of distinction in the fields of administration, management, education, academics, law, accountancy, medicine, fine arts, architecture, agriculture, science, technology, industry, and engineering such that the appointment of these persons reflects a balance across the various fields;

(f) one person from amongst the alumni of the University;

(g) two persons from the academic community of the country, other than an employee of the University, at the level of professor or principal of a college;

(h) four University Teachers (One from each tier i.e., Professor, Associate Professor, Assistant Professor and Lecturer;

(i) Nominee of the Higher Education Commission;

(j) Nominee of the Pakistan Medical and Dental Council;

(k) One Vice-Chancellor of Public Sector University to be nominated by the Chancellor;

(l) One person nominated by the Able Learning Company;

(m) One Dean to be nominated by the Chancellor; and

(n) The Registrar, who shall act as the Secretary of the Board.

(2) The numbers of the members of the Board of Governors may be increased through Statutes subject to condition that the total membership of the Board of Governors does not exceed to twenty-one and the increase is balanced, to the extent possible, across the different categories specified in sub-section (1).

(3) The Members of the Board, other than ex-officio members shall hold office for three years from the date of appointment:

Provided that members, may serve on the BoG for more than two consecutive terms.

(4) The BoG shall meet at least twice in a calendar year.

(5) Service on the BoG shall be on honorary basis:

Provided that actual expenses may be reimbursed as prescribed.

(6) The office of nominated member shall become vacant if he resigns or fails to attend three consecutive meetings of Governing body without sufficient cause of leave of absence or his nominations is changed by the Authority nominated him.

(7) In the absence of the Chancellor, meeting of the BoG shall be presided over by the ViceChancellor.

(8) Unless otherwise prescribed by this Act, all decisions of the BoG shall be taken on the basis of the opinion of a majority of the members present. In the event of the members being evenly divided on any matter the person presiding over the meeting shall have a casting vote.

(9) No act or proceedings of the board shall be invalid by reasons only of the existence of a vacancy in, or any defect in the constitution of the Board.

(10) The quorum for a meeting of the BoG shall be two thirds of its membership, a fraction being counted as one.

26. Powers and functions of the Board of Governors.―(1) The Board shall subject to the provisions of this Act, and the statutes, exercise general supervision over the affairs and management of the University.

(2) Without prejudice to the generality of the foregoing powers, the Board shall have the following powers and functions―

(a) to approve the proposed annual plan of work, the annual and revised budgets, the annual statement of accounts and the annual report;

(b) to hold, control and lay down policy for the registration of the property, finds, assets, resources and investments of the University including the approval of the sale and purchase or acquisition of immovable property;

(c) to approve financial resource development plans of the University;

(d) to oversee the quality and relevance of the University’s academic programs and to review the academic affairs of the University in general;. .

(e) to approve the appointment of the Deans, Chairperson of the teaching departments, Professors, Associate Professors, Assistant Professors and such other faculty members and officers, on the recommendations of the Selection Board as may be prescribed;

(f) to appoint Professor Emeritus on such terms and conditions as may be prescribed;

(g) to appoint the members of academic council, on the recommendations of the Vice-Chancellor;

(h) to institute schemes, directions and guidelines for the terms and conditions of appointment of all officers, teachers and other employees of the University;

(i) to approve strategic plans;

(j) to consider and pass the draft Regulations, proposed by the Academic Council:

Provided that the Board of Governors may frame a Statute or Regulation on its own initiative and approve it after calling for the advice of the Academic Council as the case my be;

(k) to make Statutes and revise them from time to time for the efficient and

effective operation of the university;

(l) to annul by order in writing the proceedings of any Authority or officer if

the Board is satisfied that such proceedings are not in accordance with the

provisions of this Act, Statutes or Regulations after calling upon such

Authority or officer to show cause why such proceedings should not be

annulled;

(m) to recommend to the Chancellor removal of any member of the Board of Governors in accordance with the provisions of this Act;

(n) to suspend, punish and remove employee (s) from service of the University whom it is empowered to appoint in the manner prescribed after due enquiry process;

(o) to remove any person from the membership of any Authority, if such person:―

(i) has become of unsound mind; or

(ii) has become incapacitated to function as member of such Authority;
or

(iii) has been convicted by a court of law for an offence involving moral turpitude; or

(iv) has been found indulging in any activity against the interest of the University;

(p) to determine the form, provide for the custody and regulate the use of the common seal of the University;

(q) to hold, control and administer the property and funds and resources of the University and raise funds upon such security as may be required under Regulations;

(r) to formulate or approve the principles, policies and plans governing the activities and operations of the University so as to facilitate teaching, research and other academic work;

(s) to create such faculties, departments and institutes and to constitute such standing committees, committees, sub-committees, councils and other administrative or academic advisory bodies as it deems necessary, and review their work;

(t) to create such academic and administrative posts as it may deem necessary and to suspend and abolish such posts;

(u) to approve, carryout, vary or cancel contracts on behalf of the University;

(v) to initiate and approve schemes for achievements of the objectives of the

University;
(w) to determine the form, and regulate the custody and use common seal of the University; and
(x) to take such initiatives as it may consider necessary for the efficient and effective management and functioning of the University.

(3) The Board may, subject to the provisions of this Act delegate all or any of the powers and functions of any Authority, officer or employee of the University at its main campus, to any Authority, committee, officer or employee at its additional campus for the purpose of exercising such powers and performing such functions in relation to such additional campus, and for this purpose the Board of Governors may create new posts or positions at the additional campus.

27. Academic Council.―There shall be an Academic Connell which shall be the Academic body of the University. The Academic Council shall consist of the following:―

(a) the Vice-Chancellor who shall be its Chairperson;

(b) Pro Vice-Chancellor (Academic and Research);

(c) Pro Vice-Chancellor (Admin and Finance);

(d) the Deans of Faculties;

(e) the Chairpersons of Teaching Departments;

(f ) Heads of the Campuses and Constituent colleges or institutes;

(g) five Professors including Emeritus Professors;

(h) one nominee of HEC not below the rank of Director General;

(i) one nominee of PM and DC

(j) two eminent scholars to be nominated by the Board;

(k) two persons of eminence to be nominated by the Chancellor;

(l) the Controller of Examinations;

(m) the Director QEC;

(n) the Director ORIC;

(o) the Controller of Examinations; and

(p) the Registrar who shall act as its Secretary.

(2) The Members of the Academic Council shall hold office for three years.

(3) The Academic Council shall meet at least once in each quarter.

