28. Powers and Functions of the Academic Council.―(1) The Academic Council shall be the principal Academic Body of the University and shall, subject to provision of this Act and Statutes, have the powers to lay down proper standards of instructions, research, publications, and examinations, and to regulate, and promote academic life of the University.

(2) Without prejudice to the generality of the foregoing powers and provisions of this Act and Statues, the Academic Council have the powers to―

(a) advice the Board on academic matters;

(b) lay down proper standards of instructions, research and examinations and to regulate and promote the academic activities of the University;

(c) regulate the conduct of teaching, research publications and examinations;

(d) approve the policies and procedures pertaining to student-related functions including admissions, expulsions, punishments, examinations and certification;

(e) approve academic programs and the policies and procedures pertaining to the quality of academic programs;

(f) approve the policies and procedures assuring quality of teaching and research;

(g) propose the Board, schemes for the constitution and organization of faculties, teaching departments, colleges or institutes and Board of studies;

(h) appoint paper setters and examiners for all examinations of the University after receiving panels of names from the relevant authorities;

(i) institute University programs for the continued professional development of University Teachers at all levels;

(J) recognize the examinations of other universities or examining bodies as equivalent to the corresponding examinations of the University;

(k) regulate the award of studentships, scholarships, exhibitions, medals and prizes;

(l) frame regulations for submission to Board;

(m) make regulations for the constitution of faculties and the teaching departments;

(n) prepare an annual report on the academic performance of the University;

(o) regulate the conduct and discipline of students and faculty;

(p) frame regulations on the recommendations of the Board of Faculties and the Boards of Studies, prescribing the courses of studies, and the syllabi for all University examinations in accordance with the policy of HEC or PM and DC and other regulatory bodies;

(q) regulate the admissions of students to the courses of studies and examinations in the University;

(r) formulate proposals, for the planning and development of teaching and research in the University;

(s) recognize the examinations of other universities or examining bodies as equivalent to corresponding examinations of the University; and

(t) perform such functions as may be prescribed by Statutes or regulations.

31. Statutes.―(1) Subject to the provisions of this Act, a statute may be made to regulate or prescribe all or any of the following matters, namely:—

(a) terms and conditions of service of employees of the University including grades or scales of pay, constitution of insurance policy, gratuity, and other fringe benefits;

(b) terms and conditions of appointments of teachers, researchers and officers;

(c) establishment of additional campuses, faculties, teaching departments, institutions, colleges and others academic units;

(d) the contents and the manner in which the annual report to be presented by the Vice-Chancellor before the Board of Governors shall be prepared;

(e) powers and duties of officers and teachers;

(f) conditions under which the University may enter into arrangements with other public or private organizations for the purposes of instruction, research and other scholarly activities;

(g) conditions for appointment of Professors Emeritus;

(h) award of honorary degrees;

(i) the University fees and other charges;

(j) efficiency and discipline of the employees of the University;

(k) acquisition and administration of properties and investments of the University;

(l) constitution, functions, and powers of the Authorities of the University; and

(m) all other matters which under this act are required to be or may be prescribed or regulated by Statutes.

(2) The draft of the Statutes shall be proposed the Board and sent for approval to the Chancellor.

(3) The Chancellor shall have the powers to assent to the statutes submitted to him or refer them back to the Board for review or reconsideration.

(4) No statute shall be valid unless approved by the Chancellor.

32. Regulations.―(1) Subject to the provisions of this Act and the Statues, the Academic Council may make Regulations, for all or any of the following matters, namely:—

(a) syllabi and courses of study and research programs leading to degrees, diplomas or certificates;

(b) schemes of studies and research, including the duration of courses, number of subjects or papers for examination;

(c) courses of studies for degrees, diplomas and certificates of the University;

(d) methodology for selection and admission of students and determination of fees and other charges for admission to various courses and examinations;

(e) admission and expulsion of students to and from the University;

(f) conduct and supervision of examinations;

(g) conditions under which a person may carry on independent research to entitle him to a degree;

(h) the conditions under which students shall be admitted to the courses and the examinations of the University and shall become eligible for the award of degrees, diplomas, and certificates;

(i) institution of fellowships, scholarships, exhibitions, medals, prizes and financial assistance;

(j) conduct of convocation and form of costumes;

(k) maintenance of discipline among students;

(l) formation of Faculties, Departments and Board of Studies; and

(m) all other academic matters under this Act or the Statutes, are to be or may be prescribed by Regulations.

(2) Regulations shall be proposed by the Academic Council and shall be submitted to the Board, which may approve them or withhold approval or refer them back to the Academic Council for reconsideration.

(3) Regulation proposed by the Academic Council shall not be effective unless it receives the approval of the Board of Governors.
